Z||000000||2FAST4U||12-31-1999||05:33 PM||||I know all of the chip companies burn upgraded chios for the 6-cyl but I can't seem to find anybody doing the 4-cyl chip.<BR>Any help would be appreciate<BR>I nobody burns chips for the 4-cyl, why not?<P>Thanks<BR>Joe||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||01-01-2000||01:36 AM||||One reason is that GM actually did a pretty good job on the 4cyl chip. They scrafed about all the performance these motors have and keep them emmisions legal.<P>There are things you can do. find a later version PROM or ECM as the case may be. That may give you a few HP.<P>You may be able to use the roller lifter setup if your motor didn't have that.<P>In reality tho the 4 cyl is built as an eco motor. in both meanings. Even in 88 it only made about 96HP and 132FP stock.<P>You might want to figure out what final gear you have in the tranny. that makes a hell of a diferance.<P>For example I'll drop to my old pinto. The original tranny was the ford c3 automatic. some jackass frid it so I converted to a stick from an eco version of the car. it's what I had at the time.<P>Later I had to change motors and replaced the tranny with one from a Pinto waggon. That tranny had a lower first gear. So low you could start out in second if you wanted. You shifted to second by 20MPH or you risked blowing the motor. I suprised allot of rice burners with that setup. <P>Diferant finals have the same effect but they also cut your top end speed. Unfortunatly you choices of tranny arelimited more in GM than Fords. Ford has made some increadibly strong gear sets. In every sence of the word strong. For all GM put into it's motors they have left tranny gearing choices to slim and nothing.||||reg||1
